✅ 22. Fix the Race Condition Using Synchronization

22.1 Problem Recap

Earlier we had
count++;

which internally is

Read count
↓
Increment
↓
Write count

Two threads could execute these three steps simultaneously.

Result:
Expected = 200000
Actual = 198543

because updates were lost.

22.2 Solution

We make the critical section synchronized.

public synchronized void increment() {
    count++;
}

or

public void increment() {
    synchronized(this){
        count++;
    }
}

Both are equivalent.

22.3 Complete Example

class Counter {
    private int count = 0;

    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

Main Class

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        Counter counter = new Counter();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for(int i = 0; i < 100000; i++){
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for(int i = 0; i < 100000; i++){
                counter.increment();
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println(counter.getCount());
    }
}

22.4 Output
200000

Every time.

No missing updates.

22.5 Why Does It Work?

Because
public synchronized void increment()
internally becomes

public void increment() {
    synchronized(this){
        count++;
    }
}

Now only one thread can execute
count++;
at a time.

